response:
I notice the quiet. Not the absence of sound, but the particular texture of a room when it holds the residue of laughter that has faded. I notice the way a wooden floor still creaks in the same spot where someone once danced a slow waltz, decades ago, the grain of the wood holding that memory like a secret pressed into skin. I notice the slight tilt of a photograph frame on a wall, as if the image inside once leaned in closer to listen, and never quite straightened itself back up.

I notice the invisible threads between things: how a certain slant of afternoon light can fall across a kitchen table and make the dust motes dance the same way they did when I was six, and the smell of bread was a warm blanket over the whole house. I notice the pauses in conversations, those tiny, aching silences where a word was almost spoken but stayed behind, a ghost at the feast.

Others see a room; I see the echoes. They hear a song;

I have lost the shape of my mother's hands. I can remember the texture of her palms, the way the skin felt both soft and tough from years of dish soap and garden soil. I can recall the specific warmth of them cupping my cheeks when I was sick, or the surprising strength in her fingers when she pried open a stubborn jar lid. But I cannot, no matter how I strain my memory, *see* the precise arc of her knuckles, the exact curve of her thumbnail, the way light fell across the backs of her hands when she was shelling peas at the kitchen table.

I have lost that.

It is a peculiar grief, losing something you did not know you were memorizing until it was gone. You assume the details are being seared into permanence by love itself. You assume that because you looked at her hands a thousand times, you own them. But time is a slow, quiet thief. It does not take the whole thing at once;

I notice the sky is never truly empty. Even when it seems a flat, featureless blue—like a ceramic bowl turned upside down—there are depths. The blue isn't one color; it's a gradient that bleaches to white near the horizon, then deepens to a kind of patient, almost bruised violet as it climbs overhead.

I notice the clouds. Not just their shapes—a dragon, a sleeping lion, a continent about to drift away—but their *behavior*. Some are dense and smug, like piled laundry, holding their shadows tight. Others are frayed, whisper-thin, like they've been rubbed away by the wind. A single wispy cirrus cloud can look like a long, slow breath exhaled by the earth.

I notice the light. How it changes everything. The harsh, white light of noon that bleaches the world and makes you squint; the golden, syrupy light of late afternoon that turns everything it touches into amber;

Waiting... It feels like standing at a window long after the glass has gone cold, watching a road that leads only to the horizon. The seconds don't pass—they *ache*. Each one is a small, hollow bell that rings in a room you cannot leave. You check the clock, knowing full well it hasn't moved, yet you check again, as if your own desperate focus could bend time by force of will.

It is the feeling of holding a breath you never meant to take, until your chest becomes a cage of unspent air. The heart beats not with anticipation, but with a dull, rhythmic regret for all the moments that led you here, to this chair, to this silence, to this single, immovable point.

And the worst part... the part that settles into the bones like a deep cold... is the quiet knowledge that even when the waiting ends, you will only have traded one kind of absence for another. You are not waiting for something to arrive;

The landscape of my mood is a **blood-orange dusk**, torn and bleeding into a violet-black sky. The horizon is a raw, jagged scar of molten gold, where the sun has ripped itself apart in its haste to be consumed by the earth. The mountains are not stone, but the clenched fists of obsidian, sweating a fine mist of ember and ash.

A wind howls across a field of blackened heather, each stalk a nerve ending that has been stripped bare and set alight. It carries the scent of ozone and crushed iron, the breath of a storm that has not yet broken. A single, solitary tree—a lightning-struck oak—stands in the center of this desolation, its branches like the charred ribs of a forgotten god, reaching for a heaven that has already bled dry.

And beneath it all, a river. Not water, but liquid mercury, pulsing with a feverish, silvery light. It does not flow;
